WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.850
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Google acquired
Stackdriver back in 2014,

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:04.970
and the suite of operations
tools in Google Cloud

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:06.840
has come a very
long way since then.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:10.025
Today, cloud monitoring is built
right into the Cloud Console,

00:00:10.025 --> 00:00:12.400
and I'm here to show you how
to set up and use monitoring

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:13.740
in your cloud projects.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.200
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.194
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.018 --> 00:00:23.810
With the integration
of monitoring features

00:00:23.810 --> 00:00:26.270
into the Cloud Console, you'll
notice that the Stackdriver

00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:27.443
brand is gone.

00:00:27.443 --> 00:00:29.360
But the functionality
you've come to depend on

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:30.375
isn't changing.

00:00:30.375 --> 00:00:33.000
You'll still be able to use all
of the features you're used to.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.932
Let's take a look at
those, and what's new.

00:00:35.932 --> 00:00:37.390
Let's start by
selecting monitoring

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:38.600
from the products menu.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.270
Instead of opening the
Stackdriver UI in a new window,

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:44.358
you get a new, better design
UI right in the console.

00:00:44.358 --> 00:00:46.150
Note that you can still
select or configure

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:47.800
the workspace in this new UI.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:49.500
The concept of
workspaces and using them

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:52.090
to aggregate monitoring data
across multiple projects

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:53.320
hasn't changed.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:54.880
Workspaces and
their host projects

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:56.620
are still how you
create and retain

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:58.912
monitoring configuration data.

00:00:58.912 --> 00:01:01.370
Let's take a look at the core
features of cloud monitoring.

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:03.453
We'll start by setting up
a VM and deploying a web

00:01:03.453 --> 00:01:04.310
server on it.

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:06.470
From there, we'll install
the monitoring agent,

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:08.810
set up an uptime check,
and configure a dashboard

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:10.670
to show us the health
of our new service.

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.045
We'll put the link to the full
steps in the episode notes

00:01:13.045 --> 00:01:15.077
so you can easily follow along.

00:01:15.077 --> 00:01:17.160
Start by creating a compute
engine virtual machine

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.010
by going to the products menu
and selecting Compute Engine.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:21.730
From there, create
a new instance.

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:23.870
You can leave most
options at default.

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:26.773
Once the instance is created,
use the SSH functionality

00:01:26.773 --> 00:01:28.440
in the console to
open a secure terminal

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:29.892
session to your new VM.

00:01:29.892 --> 00:01:31.740
We're going to use
this to do two things--

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.350
install the Apache web server
and install the monitoring

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:35.280
agent.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:38.970
First run sudo apt-get
update and sudo apt-get

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:42.450
install apache php to get
the latest package versions,

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:43.770
and install Apache.

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:46.020
Then, run these two commands
to download and install

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:47.940
the monitoring agent.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:49.650
Now that the
environment is set up,

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:51.630
we're ready to configure
cloud monitoring.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:53.270
Let's first create
an uptime check

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:54.870
that will monitor
our web service

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:56.910
and potentially notify
us if there's a problem.

00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:58.760
From the Products
menu, select Monitoring

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:00.030
and go to Uptime Checks.

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:00.782
Create a new one.

00:02:00.782 --> 00:02:02.990
You'll need to give the
machine's external IP address

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:04.338
to enter as the target here.

00:02:04.338 --> 00:02:06.630
Test the check before saving
it to make sure everything

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:08.310
is configured properly.

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:10.560
Note that this IP address
is ephemeral by default

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.160
and may change.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.700
You may want to assign a static
IP address to this machine,

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.280
if this is a service with
external users or consumers.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:20.320
From here, let's
configure a dashboard

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:22.988
so we can easily visualize the
health of our small service.

00:02:22.988 --> 00:02:25.030
We can include charts
showing us the availability

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:26.830
and performance of
our uptime check

00:02:26.830 --> 00:02:29.110
and charts showing us
infrastructure utilization

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:31.030
to get a complete picture.

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:32.740
Go to Products,
select Monitoring,

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:34.030
and click on Dashboards.

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:35.740
Then create a new dashboard.

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:38.020
To add a chart showing
uptime check failures,

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.170
select Uptime Check
URL as the resource,

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.190
and check Past as the metric.

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:45.190
Filter the data through just the
uptime check you just created

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:47.860
and use the Count False
aggregator to only show you

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:48.850
failures.

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:50.280
Once you've saved
this chart, you

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.280
can follow a similar
process to add other charts

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:53.110
to your dashboard.

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:54.280
You might want to
include a chart showing

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.113
the performance of your
service as reflected

00:02:56.113 --> 00:02:57.872
by the uptime check
latency metric.

00:02:57.872 --> 00:02:59.330
You can also include
charts showing

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:01.420
your resource utilization
of your infrastructure.

00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:03.580
Looking at metrics like
CPU utilization of your VM

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:05.945
is a great place to start.

00:03:05.945 --> 00:03:08.070
One of the new features
that's being made available

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:10.345
with this release is
the Dashboards API.

00:03:10.345 --> 00:03:12.720
You'll now be able to automate
the creation of dashboards

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:14.610
in your workspaces
and generally automate

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:16.240
more of your monitoring setup.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:18.032
We'll come back to
dashboard and monitoring

00:03:18.032 --> 00:03:19.988
automation in future episodes.

00:03:19.988 --> 00:03:21.530
Once your monitoring
is setup, you'll

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:23.113
probably want alerting
to get notified

00:03:23.113 --> 00:03:24.690
if the service goes down.

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:26.540
Since we've already
set up the monitoring,

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:28.100
we can create a
new alerting policy

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:29.940
by clicking on it in the menu.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:32.090
We can set our uptime
check as the resource type,

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:34.590
and set the metric to
make sure that it passes,

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:36.410
then set up an
email notification.

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:38.670
If you want to see more
details on how to set it up,

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:40.295
check out the link
below to the earlier

00:03:40.295 --> 00:03:42.160
video we did on alerting.

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:44.212
That wraps up our intro
to cloud monitoring

00:03:44.212 --> 00:03:45.670
and our tour of
its new integration

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:47.080
in the Cloud Console.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:49.330
We created a service,
configured a dashboard

00:03:49.330 --> 00:03:50.890
to show us its
health, and set up

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:53.018
alerting to let us know
if it has any issues.

00:03:53.018 --> 00:03:55.060
You are now ready to start
using cloud monitoring

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:56.497
in your own environment.

00:03:56.497 --> 00:03:59.080
I hope you are as excited about
the future of cloud monitoring

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:00.910
and these new features as I am.

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:02.200
That's it for this episode.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:03.867
Come back next time,
when we take a look

00:04:03.867 --> 00:04:05.650
at what's new in cloud logging.

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:06.882
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:04:06.882 --> 00:04:07.840
Stay healthy out there.

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:11.190
[MUSIC PLAYING]

